        "original_id": "b9ee852b-5781-5faf-a803-29f72f671e62",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Henry Bacon, Abraham Lincoln\n- Places: Washington, D.C.\n- Projects: Lincoln Memorial, Perry Memorial Arch\n- Topics: American national monuments, Beaux-Arts architecture\n\nFacts:\n- Henry Bacon was an American Beaux-Arts architect born on November 28, 1866.\n- The Lincoln Memorial is an American national monument built to honor Abraham Lincoln, the 16th President of the United States.\n- Henry Bacon designed the Perry Memorial Arch, which was built in 1918.\n- Henry Bacon is best remembered for the Lincoln Memorial in Washington, D.C., which was built from 1915 to 1922.\n- The Lincoln Memorial was dedicated in 1922 as one of several monuments built to honor an American president.\n- The Lincoln Memorial was Henry Bacon's final project.\n- Henry Bacon died on February 16, 1924.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b4123f1b-f4ec-5d85-b989-36de49c7e10b",
        "text": "Page: Henry Bacon. Sentence: Henry Bacon (November 28, 1866 – February 16, 1924) was an American Beaux-Arts architect who is best remembered for the Lincoln Memorial in Washington, D.C. (built 1915–22), which was his final project.\nPage: Lincoln Memorial. Sentence: The Lincoln Memorial is an American national monument built to honor the 16th President of the United States, Abraham Lincoln.\nPage: Lincoln Memorial. Sentence: Dedicated in 1922, it is one of several monuments built to honor an American president.\nPage: Perry Memorial Arch. Sentence: It was built in 1918 and designed by architect Henry Bacon.",
